after four days of rioting  south african president sula maposa  appeared on television appealing for  calm  although these may be opportunistic acts  of  looting driven by hardship and poverty  the poor and the marginalized bear the  ultimate brunt of the destruction that  is  currently underway  as i said shops have been looted and  infrastructure has been destroyed  the violence was sparked by the  imprisonment of former president jacob  zuma  he's been jailed for 15 months for  contempt of court  after not complying with an inquiry into  corruption  he's applied to the constitutional court  to review the sentence it imposed  saying it's unconstitutional many of  zuma's supporters say he's been jailed  unfairly  and they've taken to the streets to  voice their anger  but the government says what began as  support for zuma in the kwazulu-natal  province  is now being driven by crime at this  mall in soweto south of johannesburg  police are powerless as thousands of  people descend  grabbing whatever they can many of these  stores have been ransacked  and the police that we've spoken to here  have said they simply don't have enough  resources in fact they've been  pushed back by these looters some of  them stationed  within the car park as soon as any of  the looters are apprehended by police  rocks are thrown and police have to  leave  at least six people have been killed and  more than 500 arrested the unrest has  caused public services in some areas to  be suspended  including covert 19 vaccination programs  and now the military is being sent in to  help overwhelmed police  it's not certain how long the army will  have to be deployed  but there are concerns that a high  unemployment rate  worsened by a strict covet 19 lockdown  is contributing to the violence  and while boots on the ground may quell  violence for now  south africa's economic and political  problems  may run far deeper familia miller al  jazeera johannesburg
 

